  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nHigh pressure will maintain dry weather through Tuesday night as\nit tracks east across the region. Some patchy river valley fog\nis possible tonight as temperatures drop into the mid 40s to\naround 50. As the high shifts east Tuesday, wind will veer to \nthe south as 500mb heights rise. This should result in high \ntemperatures around 10 degrees warmer than today.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nRidging is expected to begin to shift southward on Wednesday, as\na cold front approaches from the Midwest and Great Lakes region.\nThe front should stall Wednesday as the flow aloft become\nparallel. Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms \nshould increase during the afternoon north of I-70 as this front\napproaches and stalls, though ridging to the south should\nmaintain generally dry weather. \n\nThe ridge should continue to drift southward Thursday, as a\nshortwave trough tracks east across the Upper Ohio Valley \nregion. Backing flow aloft with the crossing wave should push \nthe cold front across the region, with higher chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms. Shear (20kt) and instability (ML CAPE\n750 j/kg or less) are expected to remain marginal for Thursday,\nlimiting any severe weather potential. This is support by the \nlatest ML guidance, which shows minimal potential for severe \nstorms. Precipitable water does increase to around 1.8 inches, \nthough the progression of the front could also limit the locally\nheavy rainfall potential. \n\nThe front should exit the area on Friday, with diminishing\nshower chances.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nModel ensembles indicate a more zonal flow across much of the\nregion this weekend into early next week, though large\ndifferences in individual models exist. In general, disturbances\nembedded in the flow should maintain daily shower and possible\nthunderstorm chances through this period, with above average\ntemperatures.",
